From 1c09f3c8250d311aebbdf3a70aabd7b7201b9d89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 07:53:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 69/79] Add a Dark AMOLED theme alongside Light and Dark

Settings > Appearance > Theme offered System default, Light and Dark. On an
AMOLED panel the dark theme still lights every pixel, because its surfaces sit
around #1D1F26 rather than at black. This adds a fourth option that takes the
flat surfaces to true black so those pixels switch off.

ThemeType is documented as append-only because it backs a histogram, so
DARK_AMOLED goes on the end as 3 and NUM_ENTRIES becomes 4. Three places
assumed the old shape and would each have failed quietly:
GlobalNightModeStateController treated only DARK as night, so the new value
would have rendered the browser light; getThemeSettingTitle ends in a throw, so
an unhandled value crashed the settings screen; and RadioButtonGroupThemePreference
asserts on NUM_ENTRIES and indexes its button list by the enum value.

Android night mode is a binary resource qualifier, so a third dark variant
cannot be a values-* folder. It is a theme overlay instead, applied from
applyThemeOverlays alongside the existing elegant-text and density overlays.
Only the flat, full-bleed surfaces go to #000000. colorSurfaceContainerHigh is
among them because Chromium settings use it for the page background rather than
for a raised surface; measuring the running browser was the only way to find
that. colorSurfaceContainerHighest and colorSurfaceBright keep a small lift so
cards, menus and sheets stay separable from the black, and the foreground and
accent tokens are untouched so contrast is exactly the dark theme.

Dark and Dark AMOLED are both night mode, so switching between them never
changed the night flag and the activity never recreated: the overlay only
appeared after a restart. GlobalNightModeStateController now tracks the AMOLED
state separately and notifies observers when either changes, reusing the
recreate() that onNightModeStateChanged already performs.

Incognito needed its own path. Its colours live in semantic_colors_non_adaptive
and are read as resources rather than resolved as theme attributes, which is
deliberate: incognito stays dark whatever theme is chosen. A theme overlay
therefore cannot reach them, and redefining the resources is not an option
either, since a resource cannot tell Dark from Dark AMOLED. IncognitoColors is
where every incognito surface is chosen, so its five surface methods now resolve
two new attributes, colorIncognitoSurface and colorIncognitoSurfaceRaised, with
the existing resource as the fallback. Only the AMOLED overlay defines them, so
every other theme resolves nothing and behaves exactly as before. The same
attribute pair covers the incognito new tab page and the omnibox text box.

"Apply dark theme to sites, when possible" is untouched. It remains a separate
profile setting through WebContentsDarkModeController; positionCheckbox just
learns to attach it under the new option too.
